// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"lightdesign","name":"Interior lighting design","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"lightdesign-start","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: getting started","summary":"What the Interior lighting design add-on works out, how to switch it on, and how to go from a room outline and a photometry file to lux, uniformity and glare in a few steps.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["lighting","illuminance","lux","dialux","relux","photometry","ies","ldt","eulumdat","uniformity","ugr","glare","luminaire","workplane","light loss factor","en 12464","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-the-numbers-are","text":"What the numbers are","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInterior lighting design lights one room at a time. You give it the room (an outline, a ceiling height and how reflective the surfaces are), the luminaires (a maker&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.ies\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ldt\u003C/code\u003E file, or one of the built-in generic types) and where they go. It returns the illuminance on the workplane (the table-top height), how even it is, an estimate of glare (UGR), the light on walls and ceiling, and the watts per square metre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works in CadCraft and in BimCraft. Switch it on in Settings, General, Add-ons. It is off until you do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoom.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft, select a closed shape (a rectangle or polyline) and press Read again; choose it from Rooms found. In BimCraft the list holds the rooms the walls enclose. Or type the outline in millimetres. Set the ceiling height, the workplane height (0.8 m for a desk) and the reflectance of ceiling, walls and floor (a light office is about 0.7, 0.5, 0.2).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELuminaire.\u003C/strong\u003E Load the maker&#039;s file, or pick a generic type to try it. A generic type is a typical figure, not a maker&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayout.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cem\u003EPlan for a target\u003C/em\u003E finds the fewest luminaires, in an even grid, that reach the mean lux you type. \u003Cem\u003EEven grid\u003C/em\u003E and \u003Cem\u003ERows\u003C/em\u003E place them as you say.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERequirements.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick a kind of room to fill in typical targets, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECalculate.\u003C/strong\u003E You get the numbers, ticks for the requirements met, and a colour map. \u003Cem\u003EDraw in the drawing\u003C/em\u003E puts the luminaires and the lux values on layers LD-LUMINAIRE and LD-LUX (CadCraft). In BimCraft it places the luminaires in the room&#039;s level as light fixtures (one undo step; they appear in the device schedule) and writes the calculated lux as numbers in that level&#039;s plan view. \u003Cem\u003EReport\u003C/em\u003E gives a page you can print to PDF; \u003Cem\u003ECSV\u003C/em\u003E gives every grid point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-numbers-are\"\u003EWhat the numbers are\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELux are \u003Cstrong\u003Emaintained\u003C/strong\u003E: the light loss factor (0.8 by default; set it from the maintenance plan) is applied to everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe light from the luminaires is added to the light that bounces off the floor, walls and ceiling, which are treated as matt surfaces. Two independent calculations agree: a photon-tracing check of a 6 × 4 m room matches the panel to within 1.4 %.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls, ceiling and floor do not block light from each other, so an L-shaped or otherwise non-box room comes out a little too bright in its bays. Furniture, columns and partitions you list do block the direct light, windows give a daylight factor and the escape route gets an emergency check: see \u003Cem\u003EWindows, furniture and emergency lighting\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUGR\u003C/strong\u003E is an estimate from the CIE formula, from the luminaires&#039; own candela and luminous opening. Compare it with the maker&#039;s UGR table before you rely on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe kinds of room and their targets are \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical values typed from memory of EN 12464-1\u003C/strong\u003E, not checked against the edition you work to. Confirm them, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Interior lighting design lights one room at a time. You give it the room (an outline, a ceiling height and how reflective the surfaces are), the luminaires (a maker's .ies or .ldt file, or one of the built-in generic types) and where they go. It returns the illuminance on the workplane (the table-top height), how even it is, an estimate of glare (UGR), the light on walls and ceiling, and the watts per square metre. It works in CadCraft and in BimCraft. Switch it on in Settings, General, Add-ons. It is off until you do. Steps Room. In CadCraft, select a closed shape (a rectangle or polyline) and press Read again; choose it from Rooms found. In BimCraft the list holds the rooms the walls enclose. Or type the outline in millimetres. Set the ceiling height, the workplane height (0.8 m for a desk) and the reflectance of ceiling, walls and floor (a light office is about 0.7, 0.5, 0.2). Luminaire. Load the maker's file, or pick a generic type to try it. A generic type is a typical figure, not a maker's. Layout. Plan for a target finds the fewest luminaires, in an even grid, that reach the mean lux you type. Even grid and Rows place them as you say. Requirements. Pick a kind of room to fill in typical targets, or type your own. Calculate. You get the numbers, ticks for the requirements met, and a colour map. Draw in the drawing puts the luminaires and the lux values on layers LD-LUMINAIRE and LD-LUX (CadCraft). In BimCraft it places the luminaires in the room's level as light fixtures (one undo step; they appear in the device schedule) and writes the calculated lux as numbers in that level's plan view. Report gives a page you can print to PDF; CSV gives every grid point. What the numbers are Lux are maintained: the light loss factor (0.8 by default; set it from the maintenance plan) is applied to everything. The light from the luminaires is added to the light that bounces off the floor, walls and ceiling, which are treated as matt surfaces. Two independent calculations agree: a photon-tracing check of a 6 × 4 m room matches the panel to within 1.4 %. Walls, ceiling and floor do not block light from each other, so an L-shaped or otherwise non-box room comes out a little too bright in its bays. Furniture, columns and partitions you list do block the direct light, windows give a daylight factor and the escape route gets an emergency check: see Windows, furniture and emergency lighting. UGR is an estimate from the CIE formula, from the luminaires' own candela and luminous opening. Compare it with the maker's UGR table before you rely on it. The kinds of room and their targets are typical values typed from memory of EN 12464-1, not checked against the edition you work to. Confirm them, or type your own. This is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result."},{"slug":"lightdesign-glare","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: glare, uniformity and the light loss factor","summary":"How the add-on reports uniformity and glare, where its UGR figure comes from, and what the light loss factor does.","section":"reference","order":2,"keywords":["ugr","glare","uniformity","uo","light loss factor","maintenance factor","workplane","margin","grid","lux"],"headings":[{"id":"uniformity","text":"Uniformity","level":2},{"id":"glare-ugr","text":"Glare (UGR)","level":2},{"id":"light-loss-factor","text":"Light loss factor","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"uniformity\"\u003EUniformity\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUniformity is the lowest lux divided by the mean lux over the calculation grid on the workplane. The grid is spaced by the rule of EN 12464-1 (finer for a smaller room) and leaves out an edge strip (0.5 m by default) the way the standard does for the task area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"glare-ugr\"\u003EGlare (UGR)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUGR comes from the CIE 117 formula, UGR = 8 log10 (0.25 / Lb × Σ L² ω / p²). Lb is the background luminance, from the light bounced off the room. L and ω are the luminance and the size of each luminaire seen from the eye, from its candela table and luminous opening. p is the position index. Four observers sit at 1.2 m, in the middle of each side of the room, looking along the room; the worst of them is shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an estimate. It treats the luminous opening as a flat face and uses the full (initial) light output. The maker&#039;s UGR table, made the standard way, is the reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"light-loss-factor\"\u003ELight loss factor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELamps lose output and luminaires collect dirt. The light loss factor (default 0.8) multiplies every lux figure, so the numbers show what the room gives when it needs cleaning, not when it is new. Set it from the maintenance plan.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Uniformity Uniformity is the lowest lux divided by the mean lux over the calculation grid on the workplane. The grid is spaced by the rule of EN 12464-1 (finer for a smaller room) and leaves out an edge strip (0.5 m by default) the way the standard does for the task area. Glare (UGR) UGR comes from the CIE 117 formula, UGR = 8 log10 (0.25 / Lb × Σ L² ω / p²). Lb is the background luminance, from the light bounced off the room. L and ω are the luminance and the size of each luminaire seen from the eye, from its candela table and luminous opening. p is the position index. Four observers sit at 1.2 m, in the middle of each side of the room, looking along the room; the worst of them is shown. It is an estimate. It treats the luminous opening as a flat face and uses the full (initial) light output. The maker's UGR table, made the standard way, is the reference. Light loss factor Lamps lose output and luminaires collect dirt. The light loss factor (default 0.8) multiplies every lux figure, so the numbers show what the room gives when it needs cleaning, not when it is new. Set it from the maintenance plan."},{"slug":"lightdesign-daylight","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: windows, furniture and emergency lighting","summary":"How to add windows for a daylight factor, boxes that block light, and an escape route for an emergency lighting check, and what each of them leaves out.","section":"reference","order":3,"keywords":["daylight","daylight factor","window","glazing","overcast sky","furniture","partition","obstruction","emergency lighting","escape route","en 1838","lux","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"windows-and-daylight","text":"Windows and daylight","level":2},{"id":"furniture-and-partitions","text":"Furniture and partitions","level":2},{"id":"emergency-lighting","text":"Emergency lighting","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"windows-and-daylight\"\u003EWindows and daylight\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cem\u003EWindows and daylight\u003C/em\u003E type one window per line: \u003Ccode\u003Ewall, from, width, sill, head, transmittance\u003C/code\u003E in millimetres. The wall is counted from the outline: 0 is the wall from corner 0 to corner 1, 1 the next, and so on. \u003Cem\u003EFrom\u003C/em\u003E is the distance along that wall to the window&#039;s left edge, \u003Cem\u003Esill\u003C/em\u003E and \u003Cem\u003Ehead\u003C/em\u003E are the heights of its bottom and top. The transmittance (how much light the glass lets through, 0.7 if you leave it out) is the real figure from the glass maker. In BimCraft, picking a room fills the list from the model&#039;s windows; the model does not give a window&#039;s height, so \u003Cstrong\u003E1.2 m is assumed\u003C/strong\u003E: correct it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is the \u003Cstrong\u003Edaylight factor\u003C/strong\u003E on the workplane: the light inside as a percentage of the light outside, under the standard overcast sky (the CIE sky, brightest at the zenith). It gives the mean, the lowest and highest, the uniformity, the glass area and, for the light outside you choose, the lux. Type a daylight factor and the panel says how much of the room reaches it. No required value is built in: use the one your rules ask for.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat it leaves out: no sun and no clear sky, no light reflected from the ground, no buildings, trees or overhangs outside, the same share of light passing the glass at every angle, no frames, bars or curtains. The wall behind the glass reflects nothing (the opening lets light out as well). Daylight is worked out alone; it is not added to the electric light.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow it was checked: with the sky law worked by hand, a window that sees the whole sky overhead gives the full outside illuminance, an endless vertical window gives exactly half of it on a horizontal plane, and a vertical plane facing the sky gets 0.396 of it; the light that enters a room through a window equals the light the sky puts on that window, to within 0.2 % on the test room. Nobody has compared a room with another program&#039;s result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"furniture-and-partitions\"\u003EFurniture and partitions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EList boxes that block the direct light, one per line: \u003Ccode\u003Ex0, y0, x1, y1, z0, z1\u003C/code\u003E in millimetres (a partition is a thin tall box). A luminaire reaches a point only if the straight line between them crosses no box. Light that has bounced off the walls, floor or ceiling is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E blocked, so behind a big box the result is a little too bright, never too dark. Points inside a box (a column) are left out of the figures.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"emergency-lighting\"\u003EEmergency lighting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cem\u003ECheck emergency lighting\u003C/em\u003E, say which luminaires are on the emergency circuit (every n-th one), what share of their rated light they give in an emergency, and draw the escape route as points. You get the lowest and highest lux on the route&#039;s centre line and the lowest lux over the open area, and ticks against three limits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe limits (1 lx on the route, highest to lowest at most 40 to 1, 0.5 lx over the open area) are \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical values in the style of EN 1838, typed from memory and not checked against the edition you work to; they wait for a lighting engineer to confirm them.\u003C/strong\u003E Type your own. Only the direct light on the floor is counted. Signs, how long the batteries last and the switch-over time are not checked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIsolines and a 3D picture of the result.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoosing luminaires from a maker&#039;s catalogue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExterior and road lighting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDaylight from the sun, from clear skies, or with buildings outside; glare from windows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEmergency lighting signs, duration and light bounced off the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Windows and daylight Under Windows and daylight type one window per line: wall, from, width, sill, head, transmittance in millimetres. The wall is counted from the outline: 0 is the wall from corner 0 to corner 1, 1 the next, and so on. From is the distance along that wall to the window's left edge, sill and head are the heights of its bottom and top. The transmittance (how much light the glass lets through, 0.7 if you leave it out) is the real figure from the glass maker. In BimCraft, picking a room fills the list from the model's windows; the model does not give a window's height, so 1.2 m is assumed: correct it. The result is the daylight factor on the workplane: the light inside as a percentage of the light outside, under the standard overcast sky (the CIE sky, brightest at the zenith). It gives the mean, the lowest and highest, the uniformity, the glass area and, for the light outside you choose, the lux. Type a daylight factor and the panel says how much of the room reaches it. No required value is built in: use the one your rules ask for. What it leaves out: no sun and no clear sky, no light reflected from the ground, no buildings, trees or overhangs outside, the same share of light passing the glass at every angle, no frames, bars or curtains. The wall behind the glass reflects nothing (the opening lets light out as well). Daylight is worked out alone; it is not added to the electric light. How it was checked: with the sky law worked by hand, a window that sees the whole sky overhead gives the full outside illuminance, an endless vertical window gives exactly half of it on a horizontal plane, and a vertical plane facing the sky gets 0.396 of it; the light that enters a room through a window equals the light the sky puts on that window, to within 0.2 % on the test room. Nobody has compared a room with another program's result. Furniture and partitions List boxes that block the direct light, one per line: x0, y0, x1, y1, z0, z1 in millimetres (a partition is a thin tall box). A luminaire reaches a point only if the straight line between them crosses no box. Light that has bounced off the walls, floor or ceiling is not blocked, so behind a big box the result is a little too bright, never too dark. Points inside a box (a column) are left out of the figures. Emergency lighting Tick Check emergency lighting, say which luminaires are on the emergency circuit (every n-th one), what share of their rated light they give in an emergency, and draw the escape route as points. You get the lowest and highest lux on the route's centre line and the lowest lux over the open area, and ticks against three limits. The limits (1 lx on the route, highest to lowest at most 40 to 1, 0.5 lx over the open area) are typical values in the style of EN 1838, typed from memory and not checked against the edition you work to; they wait for a lighting engineer to confirm them. Type your own. Only the direct light on the floor is counted. Signs, how long the batteries last and the switch-over time are not checked. Not built yet Isolines and a 3D picture of the result. Choosing luminaires from a maker's catalogue. Exterior and road lighting. Daylight from the sun, from clear skies, or with buildings outside; glare from windows. Emergency lighting signs, duration and light bounced off the room. This is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result."}]};
